Scow-Shriek

Ingredients: SOUL JAZZ / JAZZ-FUNK + REBEL, ANGRY & AGGRESSIVE + Saxophone
Creative Catalyst: Codified by grandmothers who DJ at a floating market before dawn.

Scow-Shriek is a weaponized, high-compression collision of grease-drenched soul jazz and raw punk hostility. It takes the syncopated pocket of late-1960s jazz-funk and strips it of all lounge polish, replacing smooth warmth with overdriven alt-saxophones played to the point of reed failure, violent slap-bass hits, and unhinged vocal barks. The sonic profile is humid, claustrophobic, and relentlessly propulsive, smelling of river diesel and hot vacuum tubes.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 108–122 BPM. Dense, interlocking polyrhythmic pocket with aggressive front-of-the-beat hi-hat snapping and heavily syncopated foot-work.
- Harmonic Palette: Minor pentatonic and Dorian blues scales spiked with sharp-nine sharp-eleven altered chords, often forced into abrasive, non-functional parallel movement.
- Production Signatures: Tape-saturated room mics capture wet, ambient slapback off open water; extreme dynamic compression where the saxophone forces the bass to duck violently; hot-preamp overdrive applied directly to brass instruments.
- Dynamics: Abrupt, spastic transitions from low-slung, neck-snapping grooves to explosive, wall-of-noise brass crescendos that redline the meters without warning.

Scene & Origin Lore
The sound emerged along the mist-choked riverways of Khlong Toei during the damp hours of 4:00 AM, where elder matriarchs known as Yai-DJs rigged custom horn-loaded PA systems onto the timber hulls of floating teak scows. What began as a fierce, competitive tradition of blasting heavy soul-jazz to claim the best trading moorings morphed into a full-scale youth counterculture. Frustrated by commercial gentrification and land-side curfews, younger musicians boarded the floating rigs, bringing overdriven amplification and an ideological, anti-establishment rage. The fashion reflects this amphibious warfare: grease-stained waxed canvas coats, heavy rubber tread boots, salvaged brass jewelry, and damp silk scarves wrapped tight around the throat to protect against cold river air and exhaust fumes.

Instruments: Alto and Tenor Saxophone (run through overdrive pedals and envelope filters), Fender Rhodes Mark I (distorted through a rotary speaker), Electric Bass (slapped with heavy gauge steel strings), Custom Timber Kick Drums, Wet Hi-Hats, and Crude Engine-Piston Percussion.

For Fans Of: James Chance & The Contortions, The Comet Is Coming, Rip Rig + Panic, Sons of Kemet.

