Track 2: Tha Tien Pier Slag
- Story & Context: Deep in the hull of an abandoned rice freighter anchored near the fish docks, teenage horn player Dang recorded his confession of selling stolen brass fittings to buy his envelope filter. His reed was cracked, dripping saliva onto the damp iron floorboards while the ambient noise of morning fish sellers bled directly into the open microphone. The track captures the raw shame of a street kid trying to play himself clean through sheer physical violence against his instrument. His raspy voice cuts through the murky Rhodes chords with the painful truth: "Gold in my pocket but my lungs taste like rusted zinc."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A moody oil painting of a rusted iron ship interior lit by an industrial inspection lamp, a worn-out brass saxophone resting on a wet timber crate next to a pile of scrap metal, damp smoke hanging in the air.

Track 3: The Tide Takes the Horn
- Story & Context: Local legend claims that after the great canal raid of last autumn, Chai hurled his prized vintage Selmer saxophone into the dark currents rather than let the harbor police confiscate it. Three days later, during a low spring tide, a market seller pulled the sand-choked horn from the mud, finding its brass oxidized into a brilliant, ghostly turquoise. The closing track uses a sampled tape loop of that ruined horn’s final, wheezing note, stretched into a harsh, beautiful monument for a scene that refuses to die. Over a slow, thunderous jazz-funk pulse, a chorus of elder market women chant into the fog, "You can drown the brass but the river remembers the noise."
